{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And it shall be for a sign upon your hand.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the addition of the letter heh alludes to the five compartments in tefillin — four compartments for the head-tefillin and one compartment for the arm-tefillin. Know that the arm-tefillin is called an 'ot (sign), as it says, ״והיה לך לאות על ידך״ — 'and it shall be for a sign upon your hand'; and the head-tefillin is called 'zikaron' (remembrance), as it says, ״ולזכרון בין עיניך״ — 'and for a remembrance between your eyes.' And it is known that a thing made as a sign is always remembered, and if so, the sign is the cause of the remembrance. Therefore our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: when one lays them, he lays the arm-tefillin first and afterward the head-tefillin; and therefore Scripture said, ״והיה לאות על ידכה״ — 'and it shall be for a sign upon your hand,' placing the arm-tefillin first, and afterward <b>and for frontlets between your eyes,</b> and when one removes tefillin he removes the head-tefillin first and afterward the arm-tefillin, because if he were to remove the arm-tefillin first, as he laid them first, how would the head-tefillin, which are the remembrance, remain? And there is no remembrance except through a sign. Therefore the arm-tefillin should be first and last, for so is the sign first and last; the sign is the cause of the remembrance, but the remembrance is not the cause of the sign. From this our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: when he removes them he removes the head-tefillin first and then the arm-tefillin, as it says, ״ולטוטפת בין עיניך״ — 'and for frontlets between your eyes' — as long as they are between your eyes there shall be two." "<b>And by way of</b> the Midrash: ידכה — 'your hand' — this is the weaker hand, namely the left hand, which does not have as much strength as the right hand." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: the added heh is the final heh of the Name, and it is the attribute of Judgment below, and it is the Wisdom that was given to Solomon and is called 'lev' (heart), as it says (I Kings 3:9): ״ונתת לעבדך לב שומע״ — 'and You shall give Your servant a hearing heart,' etc. Therefore the arm-tefillin, whose commandment is placed opposite the heart. And it is the attribute called 'kallah' (bride), and the arm-tefillin is one compartment, from what is written (Song of Songs 4:9): ״אחותי כלה״ — 'my sister, my bride,' for she is unified and comprised within the thirty-two wondrous pathways of Wisdom. And in order to allude to this heh, they came in the paragraph ״והיה כי יבאך״ — five hehs are marked with tagin in the shape of ziynin, four ziynin upon each one: the heh of ״ונתנה לך״, and the two hehs of ״הקשה״, and the heh of ״והרג״, and the heh of ״ידכה״ — and all these words point to the attribute of Judgment; and this is not the rule for a Torah scroll but only for arm-tefillin. Afterward one lays the head-tefillin, which are four compartments: ״קדש לי כל בכור״, ״והיה כי יביאך״, (Deuteronomy 6:10) ״שמע״, (ibid. 11) ״והיה אם שמוע״. ״קדש לי כל בכור״ corresponds to Ḥokhmah, which is reishit, and the language of קדש indicates this, from the root of קודש (Psalms 134:2): ״שאו ידיכם קדש״, (Exodus 15:11) ״נאדר בקדש״ — which is Ḥokhmah; and likewise the language of זכר. ״והיה כי יביאך״, which speaks of the exodus from Egypt, corresponds to Binah, to contemplate the exodus from Egypt [apparently something is missing and his intent is that שמע alludes to Gedulah, and והיה אם שמוע alludes to Gevurah]. And this is the secret of the commandment of tefillin: Ḥokhmah and Binah, Gedulah and Gevurah, corresponding to the four paragraphs in tefillin, also the shin of the four heads, and Tiferet is the tefillin themselves, and this is the secret of the saying that the Holy One, blessed be He, lays tefillin; and Nezaḥ and Hod, for all the reshins of the three heads and the last one is the knot of the tefillin, and this is what they said (Exodus 33:23): ״וראית את אחורי״ — He showed him the knot of the tefillin. And the fact that the straps encircle the head alludes to the drawing down of the influx as far as the final heh, which is the knot of everything; and the fact that the straps reach as far as the navel alludes to the drawing down to the middle line and from there downward; and the fact that they are black, from the law given to Moses at Sinai, alludes to the depth of their apprehension. And the reason for placing them on the head is that it is their proper place and there is the power of the soul; and everything found in the head-tefillin is also found in the arm-tefillin in a single parchment and a single compartment, to indicate that the final heh is all comprised within it and it comprises all the attributes of above, and the knot in it alludes to Tzaddik. And the reason for placing them on the left is to indicate its reception from the left. And you need to know that arm-tefillin and head-tefillin are two commandments, and both are of one matter, and the proof that they are two commandments is that they do not impede each other — unlike the lulav and its species together with the etrog; and thus our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: head-tefillin does not impede arm-tefillin, and arm-tefillin does not impede head-tefillin — behold you have it that permission is in your hand to lay whichever one a person has; and since both allude to one matter, our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: when one lays both together one may not speak between them, and that is what they said: he who speaks between one tefillah and another commits a transgression in his hand, and they return upon him from the ranks of the warriors." "<b>And a matter</b> well known: since both of them allude to one matter, it would have been fitting that they impede each other like the lulav and etrog; and the fact that they do not impede each other is explained, as our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said: from where do we know that the Holy One, blessed be He, lays tefillin? And they said in Sefer HaBahir: to what may the matter be compared? To a king who had a beautiful vessel — sometimes he takes it on his head, sometimes he takes it on his arm. And for this reason head-tefillin and arm-tefillin do not impede each other, and from this it is made clear to you that placing tefillin on the left alludes to the attribute of Judgment which receives from the left, and its matter resembles the etrog in the left hand." "<b>However,</b> I saw from one of the sages of the Kabbalah who wrote in his composition that the reason for tefillin on the left is not like the reason for the etrog on the left, for the etrog in the left is in the hand that is proper for it, but it is not so with tefillin on the left; rather, the placing of tefillin on the left is opposite the right, as it says (Deuteronomy 33): ״מימינו אש דת למו״, and he brings a proof from what is written (Psalms 45): ״נצבה שגל לימינך בכתם אופיר״, end of quote. And you will find in the head-tefillin twenty-one times the distinctive Name, and likewise in the arm-tefillin, and the total is forty-two." "<b>And there are</b> those who hold further concerning the matter of tefillin, and they say: just as the Shekhinah dwells upon the four living creatures of the Chariot, upon the two cherubim, so too one who lays tefillin becomes a chariot for the Name, may He be exalted, and the Shekhinah dwells upon him through the four passages, upon the two specified organs within him — namely the brain and the heart: the brain from one side and the heart from the other. And this is the secret of the verse that says (Exodus 25:19): ״כרוב אחד מקצה מזה וכרוב אחד מקצה מזה״. And our Sages of blessed memory expounded: anyone who has tefillin on his head and tefillin on his arm and tzitzit on his garment is presumed not to sin, as it is said (Ecclesiastes 4:12): ״והחוט המשלש לא במהרה ינתק״. Rabbi Shimon ben Lakish said: all who lay tefillin lengthen their days, as it is said (Isaiah 38:16): ״אדני עליהם יחיו ולכל בהן חיי רוחי ותחלימני והחייני״."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 13, :notes {2 "The parenthetical editorial note beginning 'כנראה שחסר' appears in the source text (marked small); rendered within brackets as it is part of the printed edition's apparatus. The citation for שמע is given as '(שם יא)' meaning 'ibid. 11' referring back to Deuteronomy, i.e., Deuteronomy 6:4 — left as written.", 4 "Citations given as '(דברים לג)' and '(תהלים מה)' are book-only references without verse numbers; left as written.", 5 "The abbreviation ש״י expanded as 'the Name, may He be exalted' following the context of the divine name serving as the object of 'chariot'; ש״י here = שמו יתברך."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:59:13.105253061Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}